#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231788} :activity/id #uuid "ada4450f-fa33-5c1f-88fc-47bac91ee61e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050814 :topic/id #uuid "68ad7925-6faa-546c-899e-dac0bbfac1ef" :topic/math-academy-id 6722} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231788 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231986 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we will use Fourier series to solve boundary value problems with periodic forcing functions.\n\nAs before, we consider a second-order linear differential equation with constant coefficients and forcing function $f(x)$, for which the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny=y_c+y_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe main ideas are similar to those of solving IVPs:\n\n- we choose the correct form of the series for the particular solution $y_p$, and then\n\n- use boundary conditions at the endpoints to determine the constants in the homogeneous part $y_c$ of the solution.\n\nWhen $f(x)$ is periodic, we often look for $y_p$ as a Fourier series on a symmetric interval $[-L,L].$\n\nRecall that for an equation of the form $y''+\\omega^2 y=f(x)$ with no (damping) $y'$ term, the particular solution inherits the parity of $f(x)$:\n\n- If $f$ is *even*, then its Fourier series contains only cosine terms.\n\n- If $f$ is *odd*, then its Fourier series contains only sine terms.\n\nFor example, consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny''+5y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-2,2]$, where $f$ is $4$-periodic and odd. We want the appropriate Fourier series form for a particular solution $y_p$.\n\nBecause $f$ is $4$-periodic on $[-2,2]$, we have $L=2$, so the Fourier basis on this interval is\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. Since the equation has no $y'$ term, the particular solution inherits the same parity and is also odd.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for $y_p$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nLet's see more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "67a0ca11-7450-5f6a-8bdc-4aee6301f209" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14775 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "46d5d3ac-e51c-5772-ac47-f492d00757d3" :step/math-academy-id 601823 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231789}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231789 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231405 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231296 :question/id #uuid "f4a74f28-4b3b-5602-b5e5-53c8590d4a48" :question/math-academy-id "e-21887"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b3a1dcb-3788-58c3-9bf2-b0f681060ffd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050730}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455486}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of the Fourier Series For the Solution"} :step/id #uuid "68b0c11a-9b61-56e1-82c7-12c59242211e" :step/math-academy-id 601824 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231791}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231790 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231292 :question/id #uuid "b71e74ab-9cb3-5e43-94aa-52c2a926dc5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21874"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee5c94d6-9a8e-5ab4-ba71-de5ce850f903" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049324}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455504}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "23fc6608-1b88-5376-be85-f016f7a7fd7f" :step/math-academy-id 601827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231791 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231992 :tutorial/content "Recall that we are considering a second-order linear differential equation with constant coefficients and forcing function $f(x)$, for which the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny=y_c+y_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation and $y_p$ is a particular solution.\n\nThere are many types of boundary conditions (including mixed or Robin-type conditions), but in this lesson, we will focus on the two simplest cases:\n\n- *Dirichlet conditions* (values of $y$ are specified):\n\n$$\ny(a)=\\alpha\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny(b)=\\beta\n$$\n\n- *Neumann conditions* (values of $y'$ are specified):\n\n$$\ny'(a)=\\alpha\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny'(b)=\\beta\n$$\n\nA key simplification is possible if we choose our particular solution $y_p$ to satisfy the corresponding *homogeneous* boundary conditions. For any given $f(x),$ it is always possible to find such a $y_p$. \nThis drastically simplifies the computations, as the boundary conditions then determine the constants in $y_c$ directly.\n\n- *Dirichlet-type simplification:* If we choose the forcing so that\n\n$$\ny_p(a)=0\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny_p(b)=0,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\ny(a)=y_c(a)\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny(b)=y_c(b),\n$$\n\nso we can solve for the constants in $y_c$ using only the homogeneous solution.\n\n- *Neumann-type simplification:* If we choose the forcing so that\n\n$$\ny_p'(a)=0\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny_p'(b)=0,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\ny'(a)=y_c'(a)\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny'(b)=y_c'(b),\n$$\n\nso the derivative conditions determine the constants in $y_c$ directly.\n\nLet's see how this works in practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "16e90dd1-2074-5728-ad7b-e05a00c3e171" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14795 :tutorial/title "Using Boundary Conditions"} :step/id #uuid "46704bc7-b368-569c-9ed3-f228bcfd99b9" :step/math-academy-id 601983 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231790}}] :activity/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231522}]}
